WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 317: James I: The Union of the Crowns, the Gunpowder Plot of 1605, and the King James Bible

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.520
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.800
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:22.080
[Rebecca] Today, our Kings and Queens of England series examines the first King of Great Britain, a monarch of profound theological scholarship, eccentric habits, and bitter parliamentary strife, who survived the gunpowder plot and gave the world the King James Bible.

4
00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:29.440
[Rebecca] King James, I hear, is George with the extraordinary story of the first Stuart.

5
00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:41.440
[George] Born in June 1566 at Edinburgh Castle, James Charles Stewart was crowned King of Scots at thirteen months following the forced abdication of his mother, Mary Queen of Scots.

6
00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:44.000
[George] His childhood was an ordeal of terror.

7
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:48.160
[George] Four successive regents were assassinated or killed in civil war.

8
00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:59.360
[George] And the young boy was subjected to harsh pedagogical discipline by the brilliant humanist scholar George Buchanan, who beat the young king with birch rods whenever he failed his Latin lessons.

9
00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:03.120
[George] Buchanan instilled in James a profound classical learning.

10
00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:10.560
[George] James became fluent in Latin, Greek, and French, while attempting to teach him that monarchs were accountable to their people.

11
00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:13.120
[George] James developed the opposite conviction.

12
00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:26.480
[George] To shield himself from Scottish noble anarchy, he formulated the doctrine of the divine right of kings, asserting in his treatise the true law of free monarchies that kings were appointed directly by God and answered to him alone.

13
00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:40.560
[George] Surviving the Ruthven Raid kidnapping in 1582 and outmaneuvering rebellious Kirk ministers, James governed Scotland with remarkable political cunning, waiting patiently for his ultimate prize, the English crown.

14
00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:46.800
[George] James's childhood in Stirling Castle under the tutelage of George Buchanan was a trial by ordeal.

15
00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:56.720
[George] Buchanan was a ferocious Presbyterian humanist who believed that kings were mere servants of the community, subjected to deposition and death if they ruled tyrannically.

16
00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:08.440
[George] Buchanan once beat young James so severely for shouting during study that the Countess of Marr rushed in to rescue the weeping boy, demanding how Buchanan dared lay hands on the Lord's anointed.

17
00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:14.520
[George] To which Buchanan famously replied that he had whipped the king's bottom, and she was welcome to kiss it.

18
00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:26.040
[George] This harsh pedagogical upbringing instilled in James a lifelong hatred of Presbyterian Presbyteries and a profound, almost theological obsession with the absolute divine right of kings.

19
00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:39.720
[George] When Queen Elizabeth I died in March 1603, Sir Robert Carey rode non-stop four hundred miles from London to Edinburgh in sixty hours to present James with Elizabeth's sapphire ring, greeting him as King of England.

20
00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:46.680
[George] James's journey south in April 1603 was a month-long victory progress of breathtaking pageantry.

21
00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:53.800
[George] English noblemen greeted him with lavish feasts, gifts of gold plate, and hunts in magnificent deer parks.

22
00:02:53.800 --> 00:03:02.600
[George] For James, leaving the impoverished, rugged landscape of Scotland for the fertile, wealthy plains of England was like arriving in the promised land.

23
00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:13.400
[George] Crowned at Westminster in July 1603, James immediately proclaimed himself King of Great Britain, creating the first Union Jack flag by fusing the Red Cross of St.

24
00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:15.160
[George] George with the white cross of St.

25
00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:16.040
[George] Andrew.

26
00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:22.840
[George] But London was reeling from a devastating outbreak of bubonic plague that killed thirty thousand citizens that summer.

27
00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:26.680
[George] Furthermore, English courtiers were bemused by their new king.

28
00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:33.000
[George] James spoke with a broad Scottish brogue, wore padded doublets for fear of dagger assassination.

29
00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:41.240
[George] Slobbered when drinking wine, and rarely washed his hands, presenting an earthy contrast to the majestic theatricality of Elizabeth.

30
00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:46.720
[George] James's progress south in 1603 was an intoxicating revelation of English wealth.

31
00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:55.040
[George] At Theobald's in Hertfordshire, Robert Cecil entertained the king with fireworks, tournaments and feasts that cost thousands of pounds.

32
00:03:55.040 --> 00:04:02.320
[George] James was so enamoured with the grand estate that he persuaded Cecil to exchange Theobald's for the royal palace of Hatfield.

33
00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:08.960
[George] Yet James's personal eccentricities scandalized English courtiers accustomed to Elizabeth's majestic dignity.

34
00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:11.840
[George] James hated the sight of drawn swords.

35
00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:27.120
[George] Often vomited after heavy drinking of sweet Greek wine, embraced his handsome male courtiers with excessive public intimacy, and rode his horses with his legs loosely flapping in the stirrups, earning the satirical description of the wisest fool in Christendom.

36
00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:32.320
[George] Religious disillusionment soon brought the new regime to the edge of total annihilation.

37
00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:41.600
[George] English Catholics, hoping James would grant religious tolerance, were enraged when the king enforced harsh recusancy fines and banished Catholic priests.

38
00:04:41.600 --> 00:05:04.240
[George] In early 1604, a charismatic Catholic gentleman named Robert Catesby devised an apocalyptic conspiracy, blowing up the House of Lords during the state opening of Parliament when the King, Queen, Prince of Wales, Lords, Commons, and Bishops were all gathered in one room, wiping out the entire Protestant governing establishment in a single second.

39
00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:14.640
[George] The plotters leased a cellar directly beneath the House of Lords, concealing thirty-six barrels containing two and a half tons of gunpowder beneath piles of firewood and iron bars.

40
00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:22.640
[George] The plan was betrayed when a conspirator sent an anonymous warning letter to Catholic peer Lord Monteagle, urging him to stay away.

41
00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:33.400
[George] On the midnight of November 4, 1605, royal magistrates searched the undercroft, discovering Guy Fawkes lurking with matches, a dark lantern, and touchwood.

42
00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:37.720
[George] Under agonizing torture on the rack in the tower, Fawkes confessed.

43
00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:45.080
[George] The plotters were hunted down, and their public executions inaugurated the annual British celebration of Bonfire Night.

44
00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:49.640
[George] The gunpowder plot was an existential threat of unprecedented scale.

45
00:05:49.640 --> 00:06:08.920
[George] The thirty-six barrels of powder in the undercroft beneath the House of Lords were sufficient to completely obliterate the entire palace of Westminster, Westminster Abbey, and surrounding streets, shattering windows across London and incinerating the king, the royal family, and the entire English governing establishment.

46
00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:16.680
[George] Guy Fawkes, operating under the alias John Johnson, proved to be an iron fanatic, under initial interrogation.

47
00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:24.440
[George] He insolently informed the Scottish king that the gunpowder was intended to blow you Scotch beggars back to your native mountains.

48
00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:32.680
[George] Only the agonizing torture of the rack, stretching his joints until he could barely hold a pen, forced his shaking signature upon the confession.

49
00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:39.160
[George] James's most enduring and monumental legacy to world civilization was forged in theological debate.

50
00:06:39.160 --> 00:06:49.160
[George] In January 1604, James convened the Hampton Court Conference to address Puritan petitions for church reform, when Puritans attacked bishoprics.

51
00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:52.440
[George] James exploded with his famous royal maxim.

52
00:06:52.440 --> 00:07:11.400
[George] No bishop, no king, however, when Puritan scholar John Reynolds proposed a new uniform English translation of the Bible to replace the flawed Bishop's Bible and the Calvinist Geneva Bible, whose marginal notes James despised as seditious, James enthusiastically embraced the project.

53
00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:20.880
[George] Forty-seven of the realm's foremost scholars, linguists, and clergymen were organized into six committees meeting at Westminster, Oxford, and Cambridge.

54
00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:32.320
[George] Working from original Hebrew, Aramaic, and Greek texts, the translators sought what they called a well-tuned symbol, reading their translations aloud to test their acoustic cadence.

55
00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:36.240
[George] Published in 1611 by royal printer Robert Barker.

56
00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:50.160
[George] The authorized King James Bible became the supreme literary monument of the English language, shaping religious worship, literature, and everyday speech across the English speaking world for centuries with its sublime prose poetry.

57
00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:55.680
[George] The King James Bible translation was an extraordinary feat of scholarly collaboration.

58
00:07:55.680 --> 00:08:08.560
[George] Forty-seven translators, working in six committees at Westminster, Oxford, and Cambridge under the leadership of Bishop Lancelot Andrews, translated every book of Scripture with breathtaking literary care.

59
00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:18.880
[George] Each translator produced his own version, which was reviewed by the committee, read aloud to ensure rhythmic acoustic cadence, and revised until consensus was reached.

60
00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:30.320
[George] By avoiding sectarian marginal notes and preserving majestic cadence, producing unforgettable phrases like the Valley of the Shadow of Death, a soft answer turneth away wrath.

61
00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:38.480
[George] And Pearls Before Swine, the 1611 authorized version became the foundational prose masterpiece of world literature.

62
00:08:38.480 --> 00:08:43.520
[George] James was unique among British monarchs for authoring major scholarly treatises.

63
00:08:43.520 --> 00:08:57.440
[George] He wrote a counterblast to tobacco in 1604, a remarkably prescient denunciation of smoking, describing it as a custom loathsome to the eye, hateful to the nose, harmful to the brain, and dangerous to the lungs.

64
00:08:57.440 --> 00:09:00.680
[George] But his darkest intellectual obsession was witchcraft.

65
00:09:00.920 --> 00:09:13.080
[George] In 1589, when violent North Sea tempests repeatedly prevented his bride Anne of Denmark from sailing to Scotland, James became convinced that demonic Covens had raised the storms.

66
00:09:13.080 --> 00:09:28.440
[George] Personally interrogating suspected witches at Holyrood, including Agnes Sampson, James published his terrifying treatise Demonology in 1597, defending the death penalty for witches and asserting that Satan's agents walked among mankind.

67
00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:38.360
[George] In 1604, his first English Parliament passed the Witchcraft Act, expanding the felony to include invoking evil spirits or desecrating corpses.

68
00:09:38.360 --> 00:09:49.160
[George] This dark royal obsession directly inspired William Shakespeare to incorporate the Weird Sisters and demonic incantations into Macbeth to flatter his royal Scottish patron.

69
00:09:49.160 --> 00:10:02.680
[George] James's obsession with witch hunting in Scotland resulted in the horrific North Berwick witch trials of 1590, in which over a hundred suspected witches were tortured with thumbscrews, iron boots, and sleep deprivation.

70
00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:07.960
[George] Sampson was alleged to have thrown a cat into the sea to raise storms against the king's bride.

71
00:10:07.960 --> 00:10:15.320
[George] In demonology, James argued that women were especially prone to witchcraft because Eve had succumbed to the serpent in Eden.

72
00:10:15.320 --> 00:10:31.800
[George] Yet in his later years in England, James became increasingly skeptical, using his razor sharp intellect to personally debunk fraudulent teenage exorcists and exposed impostors who claimed to be possessed, demonstrating the curious contradictions of his complex mind.

73
00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:41.560
[George] James's court was increasingly scandalous, defined by his passionate infatuations with handsome young male favourites upon whom he showered titles and wealth.

74
00:10:41.560 --> 00:10:48.320
[George] His first major favourite was Robert Carr, a handsome Scottish youth whom James created Earl of Somerset.

75
00:10:48.320 --> 00:11:02.880
[George] But Carr's court supremacy collapsed in 1615 in the infamous Overberry poisoning scandal, when Carr and his wife Frances Howard were convicted of poisoning poet Sir Thomas Overberry in the tower with poisoned tarts and mercury sublimate.

76
00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:08.880
[George] Carr was replaced by George Villiers, a dazzlingly beautiful young Leicestershire nobleman.

77
00:11:08.880 --> 00:11:23.840
[George] James was utterly infatuated with Villiers, creating him Duke of Buckingham, the highest noble rank in the realm, and publicly kissing him, declaring before the Privy Council, You may be sure that I love the Earl of Buckingham more than anyone else.

78
00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:27.120
[George] Christ had his John, and I have my George.

79
00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:37.360
[George] Buckingham monopolized royal patronage, selling peerages and offices, earning the deep and bitter hatred of England's traditional aristocracy and parliamentary leaders.

80
00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:43.120
[George] The Overberry poisoning scandal was the greatest court sensational murder of the Jacobean era.

81
00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:53.280
[George] Francis Howard, wanting to divorce the Earl of Essex to marry Robert Carr, procured arsenic, mercury sublimate, and can therides from London apothecaries.

82
00:11:53.280 --> 00:11:59.040
[George] Slipping the poisons into jellis and tarts served to Overberry in the tower until he died in agony.

83
00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:05.360
[George] When the conspiracy unraveled, Carr and Howard were condemned to death, though James spared their lives.

84
00:12:05.360 --> 00:12:23.040
[George] George Villiers, Duke of Buckingham, exploited James's emotional infatuation to build an unassailable monopoly over royal patronage, amassing an astronomical personal fortune while alienating the House of Commons and leading disastrous military expeditions to Cadiz and La Rochelle.

85
00:12:23.040 --> 00:12:34.840
[George] James's unyielding belief in divine right clashed violently with an increasingly assertive House of Commons, which insisted on its historic privileges of free speech and control over taxation.

86
00:12:34.840 --> 00:12:49.000
[George] When Parliament drew up its famous protestation in 1621, asserting its ancient rights, James summoned the clerk, tore the offending page out of the House of Commons journal with his own hands, and dissolved Parliament in fury.

87
00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:59.480
[George] The French King Henry IV famously described James as the wisest fool in Christendom, a sovereign of dazzling book learning who was hopelessly blind to political reality.

88
00:12:59.480 --> 00:13:06.040
[George] By 1625, James was crippled by severe arthritis, gout, and kidney failure.

89
00:13:06.040 --> 00:13:16.520
[George] On March 27, 1625, 58-year-old King James I died at Theobald's house in Hertfordshire, holding the hand of his son, Prince Charles.

90
00:13:16.520 --> 00:13:25.240
[George] James had succeeded in avoiding catastrophic foreign wars, united Britain under a single crown, and gifted the world the King James Bible.

91
00:13:25.240 --> 00:13:38.360
[George] But by asserting absolute divine right while failing to resolve the fundamental constitutional struggle with Parliament, James left his son Charles I with the combustible materials that would soon ignite the English civil war.

92
00:13:38.360 --> 00:13:47.000
[George] James's clashes with Parliament over royal expenditure and foreign policy established the ideological battle lines that would destroy his son.

93
00:13:47.000 --> 00:14:04.760
[George] When James lectured Parliament that kings were gods on earth, and that it was sedition to dispute what a king may do in the height of his power, the Commons responded with the great protestation of 1621, asserting that their privileges were the ancient and undoubted birthright of the subjects of England.

94
00:14:04.760 --> 00:14:20.880
[George] When James died in March 1625, he left a realm at peace, but a monarchy financially exhausted, politically isolated, and burdened with an intractable constitutional crisis that would culminate on the scaffold at Whitehall 24 years later.

95
00:14:21.440 --> 00:14:25.360
[Rebecca] Thank you for joining us for this episode of Most Fascinating Stories.

96
00:14:25.360 --> 00:14:41.920
[Rebecca] If you enjoyed this chapter of James I, The Union of the Crowns, The Gunpowder Plot of 1605, and the King James Bible, subscribe to the show, share it with a fellow history lover, and tune in next Tuesday for our next journey into history's most extraordinary events.

97
00:14:41.920 --> 00:14:44.560
[Rebecca] Until next time, I'm Rebecca.

